[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "655616af-26c7-5045-9c00-22ddde224244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨1,1,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361294} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4f75ae-d6ad-5a3c-91cb-30e81c920d26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}⟨0,1,t⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361390} {:answer/id #uuid "991991bb-1478-585f-92cd-d50366a4e536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨-2,t,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361520} {:answer/id #uuid "b70d3730-f7e4-550d-8e7c-aaa70734dfad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨1,0,0⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361653} {:answer/id #uuid "db90d517-2caf-5377-83c7-89e38b72b976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db90d517-2caf-5377-83c7-89e38b72b976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨0,0,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361814} :answer-field/id #uuid "4efaf2f0-5db1-5f94-9975-e72a5434ef99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38a9961c-14c0-5e12-92b6-0fd05f67f8cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-99504" :question/problem "Find the binormal vector to the curve $r(t)$ at an arbitrary point, given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $T$ denotes the unit tangent vector." :question/worked-solution "The binormal vector is given by\n\n$$\nB(t)=T(t)\\times N(t),\n$$\n\nwhere the principal normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4})}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}[\\text{ }\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,i-\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,j+\\left|\\begin{aligned}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k\\text{ }] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171690 :question/id #uuid "b48b1e81-1bfa-5236-b43d-edf2c44503d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11000" :question/problem "If $\\sin x = \\dfrac{1}2$, then what is $-2\\sin(x+6\\pi)-3$?" :question/worked-solution "The period of $y = \\sin x$ is $2\\pi$. Therefore, for any integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\sin{(x+2n\\pi)}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sin x &= \\sin (x + 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot π) \\\\\n&= \\sin (x + 6π)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if\n\n$$\n\\sin{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\sin{(x+6\\pi)} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\sin (x + 6π) - 3 &= -2(\\frac{1}{2}) - 3 \\\\\n&=-1 - 3 \\\\\n&=-4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171691 :question/id #uuid "5aeb80cf-cd16-5823-977f-60dbadbdab73" :question/math-academy-id "e-11001" :question/problem "If $\\sin{x} = -\\dfrac{4}{7}$, then what is the value of $\\sin(-x + 16 \\pi)$?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\sin{x}\n$$\n\n![](images/08/08d442eff9febe44bd39e6c15c998d76d56d17bbb7a676d2d511771479bb2f29.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\sin{x}$ is $2\\pi$.\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\sin{x}$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n$\\sin(-x) = -\\sin{x}$.\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x + 16π)|= \\sin (-x + 2π \\cdot 8); = \\sin (-x)$.\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\sin{x}$, we have\n$\\sin (-x)|=-\\sin x; = - (-\\frac{4}{7}); = \\frac{4}{7}$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\sin(-x + 16\\pi) = \\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171744 :question/id #uuid "5f47fd84-fda1-5eba-b32e-53b07a391b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-14748" :question/problem "Do the following sets have the same cardinality? If so, why?\n\n$$\nA = \\mathbb{N}, \\qquad B = \\left\\{1,\\, \\dfrac12,\\,  \\dfrac14,\\,  \\dfrac18,\\,  \\ldots \\right \\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Two sets $A$ and $B$ have the *same cardinality* if there exists a bijection between $A$ and $B.$\n\nNotice that the elements of $B$ form an (infinite) geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=\\dfrac12{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{4})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $n$th term of our geometric sequence, denoted $a_n,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =1\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express $B$ in set-builder notation as \n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{  \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\,  \\Bigg |\\:  n \\in \\mathbb{N}  \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, consider the function $f: A \\to B$ that maps the underlying sets as follows: \n\n| $A$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $\\cdots$ | $n$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $B$ | $1$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1}$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nIn general, for each $n \\in A,$ we have $f(n) =   \\left(\\dfrac12\\right)^{n-1} \\in B.$\n\nNotice that $f$ is described in such a way that it is bijective. Indeed, it's\n\n- injective since $f(n) \\neq f(m)$ whenever $n \\neq m,$ and\n\n- surjective since each element in $B$ (the second row) has a pre-image in $A$ (the first row).\n\nTherefore, $|A| = |B|,$ i.e., the sets have the same cardinality."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171767 :question/id #uuid "008dea21-d434-524e-ba21-aefa78b7d5f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-15122" :question/problem "Consider a normal population with unknown mean and standard deviation. Let $I_1$ be a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ obtained from a sample of size $n_1$ using as an estimate for the standard deviation $s_1=5$, calculated from a previous study.\n\nSuppose we require a second $98\\%$ confidence interval $I_2$ to have the same length as $I_1$, obtained using a different estimate $s_2=3$. Find the relative size of the second sample $n_2$ we should use to construct $I_2$.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z \\sim N(0,1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_p$ such that $P(Z > z_p) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_p$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1$, the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\overline{x} \\pm \\overbrace{z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}}^{\\large \\textrm{margin of error}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $\\!\\overline{\\,x}$ is the sample mean,\n- $n$ is the sample size,\n- $\\sigma$ is the population standard deviation, and\n- $P(Z > z_{\\alpha/2}) = \\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}$ and $Z \\sim N(0,1)$.\n\nLet $\\varepsilon_1$ and $\\varepsilon_2$ be the margins of error of $I_1$ and $I_2$, respectively. Since we want $I_2$ to be equal to $I_1$, we must have\n\n$$\n\\varepsilon_1 = \\varepsilon_2\n$$\n\nThen, we have\n\n$$\nz_{0.025} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_1}{\\sqrt{n_1}} = z_{0.01} \\cdot \\dfrac{s_2}{\\sqrt{n_2}}\n$$\n\nSolving for\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{n_2}{n_1}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}} \\\\\n(\\frac{\\sqrt{n_{2}}}{\\sqrt{n_{1}}})^{2} &= (\\frac{z_{0.01} \\cdot s_{2}}{z_{0.025} \\cdot s_{1}})^{2} \\\\\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{z_{0.01}^{2} \\cdot s_{2}^{2}}{z_{0.025}^{2} \\cdot s_{1}^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\ns_1 = 5, s_2 = 3, z_{0.01} = 2.326, z_{0.25} = 1.960\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{n_{2}}{n_{1}} &= \\frac{2.326^{2} \\cdot 3^{2}}{1.960^{2} \\cdot 5^{2}} \\approx 0.507\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nn_1\\approx 1.97 n_2\n$$\n\nTherefore, $n_2$ must be $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{smaller}}$ than $n_1$ by a factor of approximately $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1.97}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171809 :question/id #uuid "587f0594-4911-5ad7-9f1a-bd33cfbad3fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5109" :question/problem "Which expression is missing in the following equation?\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{\\left[ \\phantom{3 \\choose 3}\\right]}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\displaystyle{\\color{red}{4} \\choose {\\color{blue}3}},$ we need to find the number that is located in row $\\color{red}4$ and position $\\color{blue}3$ of Pascal's triangle. \n\n**Important:** Remember that when counting the rows and positions in Pascal's triangle, we always start counting from zero!\n\n- The top row of Pascal's triangle is row $0.$\n\n- The first position of each row (the one of the left) is position number $0.$\n\nThe location of $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ in Pascal's triangle is highlighted below.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{0}{0}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{1}{0}) &  & (\\frac{1}{1}) \\\\  &  &  &  &  & (\\frac{2}{0}) &  & (\\frac{2}{1}) &  & (\\frac{2}{2}) \\\\  &  &  &  & (\\frac{3}{0}) &  & (\\frac{3}{1}) &  & (\\frac{3}{2}) &  & (\\frac{3}{3}) \\\\  &  &  & (\\frac{4}{0}) &  & (\\frac{4}{1}) &  & (\\frac{4}{2}) &  & (\\frac{4}{3}) &  & (\\frac{4}{4}) \\\\  &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3}$ is the sum of the two closest numbers located in the previous row. So\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{4 \\choose 3} = \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 2} + \\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\displaystyle{3 \\choose 3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171810 :question/id #uuid "9f9efb07-33a0-5a28-8eef-656a3c00a200" :question/math-academy-id "e-5231" :question/problem "Determine the value of $\\displaystyle{5 \\choose 4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial coefficient $\\displaystyle{n \\choose r}$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{n \\choose r} = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,  r!}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $n = 5$ and $r = 4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{5}{4}) & =\\frac{5!}{(5-4)!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 4!}{1!\\cdot 4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{4!}}{1\\cdot \\cancel{4!}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171824 :question/id #uuid "a7f9f74b-e213-5f38-8cb2-b9352ee515ad" :question/math-academy-id "e-7471" :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $5x^2 -10x - 4y - 7 = 0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^2 = 4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{2}-10x-4y-7 & =0 \\\\ 5{x}^{2}-10x & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[{x}^{2}-2x] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5[(x-1)^{2}-1] & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2}-5 & =4y+7 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+7+5 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4y+12 \\\\ 5(x-1)^{2} & =4(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =\\frac{4}{5}(y+3) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{5})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k) = (1,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171836 :question/id #uuid "58f6a3e7-e867-576a-ad84-0debbbc38d90" :question/math-academy-id "e-8381" :question/problem "What type of quadratic curve is defined by the following equation?\n\n$$\n3x_1^2 + 2x_1 - x_2 =\\dfrac{5}{3}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the variable $x_1\\mathbin{:}$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3({x}_{1}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{1}+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2})-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-3{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{3}-{x}_{2} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ 3{({x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2} & ={x}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, performing the change of variables\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &={x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{3}, \\\\ {y}_{2} &={x}_{2}+2,\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nwe get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1}^{2} & ={y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the positive $y_2$-axis."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171871 :question/id #uuid "5d084f86-9874-5bdd-a416-dba742d7e4e5" :question/math-academy-id "e-8952" :question/problem "Calculate $\\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{5}{2} \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\n\\Gamma(z+1) = z \\Gamma(z), \\qquad \\Gamma \\left( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) = \\sqrt{\\pi}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) & =\\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171895 :question/id #uuid "f6f11512-c37f-5922-b642-5290003b1d91" :question/math-academy-id "e-9492" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd1ba2536264e122c84377a1892c3ca86dd8f273b2eab9ba0e2c53eeb504ef65.png)\n\n  \n\nFind a definite integral with respect to $\\theta$ that's equivalent to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-\\sin{\\theta}\\,$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq  \\pi,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-\\sin{\\theta},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = -\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171896 :question/id #uuid "6753365e-9915-5be9-9a6d-d4c40b2caea0" :question/math-academy-id "e-9493" :question/problem "![](images/a5/a55339eae9b193813ab696538c85696ad5f4267e2e31513bdb2c8998cd711311.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\,\\textrm d s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta)$ for $0 \\leq \\theta \\leq {\\pi},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s = L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r = r(\\theta)$ for $\\theta_1 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\theta_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL=\t\\int_C \\,\\textrm d s =  \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2} \\sqrt{r^2 +\\left(\\frac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta}\\right)^2 }\\,\\textrm{d}\\theta.\n$$\n\nSince $r=2(1+\\sin\\theta),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}r}{\\textrm{d}\\theta} = 2\\cos{\\theta}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+4{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{2+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})=1,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRewriting our integral using the above identities, we can evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2}))}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})|\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}{[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))-(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}((0+1)-(-1+0)) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171909 :question/id #uuid "3912c570-ae90-5d82-b7ee-3adc26f7f4af" :question/math-academy-id "e-9885" :question/problem "The displacement $\\mathbf r,$ in meters, of a particle $P$ of mass $3\\,\\textrm{kg}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r =  \\langle 3\\cos{t}-\\sin{t},\\: 2\\cos{t}\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\gt 0$ is the time in seconds. Calculate the resultant force acting on the particle when $t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s.$" :question/worked-solution "Newton’s second law states that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F = m \\mathbf a,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $\\mathbf F$ is the *resultant* force, $m$ is the mass, and $\\mathbf a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo compute the acceleration vector, we first differentiate $\\mathbf r$ to get the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate the velocity vector to get the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resultant force $\\mathbf F$ at time $t$ equals\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot ⟨-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $t = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})-9\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,-6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\,\\frac{6\\sqrt{2}}{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6\\sqrt{2},\\,3\\sqrt{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the resultant force acting on $P$ when $t=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{4}\\,\\textrm s$ equals $\\left\\langle 6\\sqrt{2},\\: 3\\sqrt{2}\\right\\rangle\\,\\textrm N.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57f6601b-563c-5b31-a64c-7ec8c55ad32b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432618} {:answer/id #uuid "680dd59f-269a-5239-be6c-db7639bc6aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432625} {:answer/id #uuid "6c60091c-6a50-58f6-8506-d0161e3cbf8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{4}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432626} {:answer/id #uuid "afc4fe46-ebb9-53be-a4db-c19fc81cfcc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432644} {:answer/id #uuid "e8402d97-f15b-5b34-be19-f1b09c6f80c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{6}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "680dd59f-269a-5239-be6c-db7639bc6aff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186432625} :answer-field/id #uuid "15d1edb6-5522-5f3e-9a34-d393c62a2da0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "985175a0-3817-5f02-8236-9b18519858f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-105737" :question/problem "Evaluate $Γ(\\frac{7}{2}).$" :question/worked-solution "To evaluate the gamma function at multiples of one-half, we use the following recursive formula:\n\n$$\nΓ(z+1)=zΓ(z),\\,Γ(\\frac{1}{2})=\\sqrt{\\pi }\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Γ(\\frac{7}{2}) & =\\frac{5}{2}Γ(\\frac{5}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}Γ(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{8}\\sqrt{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172235 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b367d3ad-bb24-5bad-ab86-f66b9d3043d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168274} {:answer/id #uuid "c5539f4c-97cb-552d-b15f-a690555d4c23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168275} {:answer/id #uuid "0a160ea2-163a-54a0-bce8-410058de71ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168276} {:answer/id #uuid "56d47025-c197-5474-8213-65096f747ac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168277} {:answer/id #uuid "73bb7a3d-3df6-530c-910a-432596a099e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}\\sqrt{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168278}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d6769a1d-3919-5d01-a653-0366af50c7b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e482d16-749d-58c9-8ef6-b26f04eebbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-107764" :question/problem "What is $Γ(\\frac{1}{2}) + Γ(\\frac{3}{2})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b37f815-69e9-5f1e-9c62-4a6ed82da6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250924} {:answer/id #uuid "4a368124-0d01-5818-a452-6cd091e09815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251170} {:answer/id #uuid "6a26be98-0aef-5a51-bca6-58adc676c0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251425} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee6b668-ccb3-53dc-86e1-9666b077a249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251457} {:answer/id #uuid "a8408a74-d250-5b83-97a6-711d103381bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8408a74-d250-5b83-97a6-711d103381bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252007} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef0e9e67-bf6e-5236-8fc8-d1ae8a6d7a36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "330d0d52-2b13-5441-8452-b279bf17bf67" :question/math-academy-id "q-11883" :question/problem "Calculate the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard equation of an upward- or downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nTo write the given equation in this form, we need to group the $x$-terms on the left-hand side and complete the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+6x+4y+15 & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+6x & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[{x}^{2}+2x] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3[(x+1)^{2}-1] & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2}-3 & =-4y-15 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-15+3 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4y-12 \\\\ 3(x+1)^{2} & =-4(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}(y+3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})(y+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k)=(-1,-3).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0a4588a-a70a-5752-b2c8-01cc42d39edf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/271bcaebb02bc573b2d6c3b00758387ff1a49787270c137f7e69ed3223e7de2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168580} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb7e952-7f56-56d6-875d-d7fd08e13aac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168581} {:answer/id #uuid "5f0e3626-c59b-53de-817d-5c6c8128a918" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21fc0f3e9a2d5100421953ef2270de9d8a99fb395d761b4eb9a3f9f0e1889ac3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168582} {:answer/id #uuid "7951f9ae-9706-5dfa-82a5-3cfe79b73cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d3b3c7f1a3b85e40cec4392b4c487c5423636837ea2672c6cdcd6fdeb2b0cc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168583} {:answer/id #uuid "7f0edf41-9f7f-55ea-993a-529370820e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f87c6b14dba3adad00c49362df7ba03bcbb6f990eba0302bcd10d484f15b070.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168584}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d97d5ed5-35ab-5c3c-88ac-a04ef2adefb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168579} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f19d19af-b717-5081-be6e-62e25a75c568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d3b3c7f1a3b85e40cec4392b4c487c5423636837ea2672c6cdcd6fdeb2b0cc7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380707} {:answer/id #uuid "b54cebe4-6aa6-502d-9b98-b267d3c2bb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380708} {:answer/id #uuid "480fd893-6a75-5704-903f-fe9945ae2257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/27/271bcaebb02bc573b2d6c3b00758387ff1a49787270c137f7e69ed3223e7de2b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380709} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e4571a-7acf-5877-a904-62367ce6ac1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1f/1f87c6b14dba3adad00c49362df7ba03bcbb6f990eba0302bcd10d484f15b070.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380710} {:answer/id #uuid "e08cd2a9-0dc9-5455-b4d2-241cc812bcb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/21fc0f3e9a2d5100421953ef2270de9d8a99fb395d761b4eb9a3f9f0e1889ac3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b54cebe4-6aa6-502d-9b98-b267d3c2bb70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380708} :answer-field/id #uuid "08090d20-3972-5c5d-a3b3-2558a12e085f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59a69c0f-c2af-5b88-9b38-1fbc42cfdc3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-122756" :question/problem "Which of the following diagrams represents the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi y}{2})\\,j$ at the indicated points?" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the vector field at the points $(-3,1),(-2,-1),$ and $(4,-4).$\n\nTo sketch the vector field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the function $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi y}{2}\\,j:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(-3,1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-3))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (1)}{2})\\,j=-2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ F(-2,-1) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (-2))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ F(4,-4) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi (4))\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi (-4)}{2})\\,j=2\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now draw our vectors. At each point, we draw the vector associated with that point, placing the tail end of the vector on the point itself. This gives the following diagram.\n\n![](images/33/33c6c3680f3cab38ffd1508fad160948f3bc1159c01dc287b3fe113c0cf89875.png)"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172287 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} {:answer/id #uuid "0560e91f-841f-5133-b19f-414819f58310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}(2{e}^{2\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362985} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c2c04d-6401-5b56-9894-aeead7dbe986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{e}^{2\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362986} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc5fcaa-258a-5aeb-90ce-1bd2af6582b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{2\\pi }+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362987} {:answer/id #uuid "a210ea76-975d-59a6-8399-df6d3b035510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f429c0-4106-53ea-9c7e-5a3e5c69557c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362984} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b62b53e-864d-5c41-bf41-a59840c727e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f2b06f7c-408a-5dfe-b281-0fde1f80e146" :question/math-academy-id "q-123354" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d33f9b851125443ff738fa4c3aedcbeaf8a202d05caa06747e74262c0ae89bd9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={e}^{2\\theta }\\,$ for $0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={e}^{2\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2{e}^{2\\theta }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}+{(2{e}^{2\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{e}^{4\\theta }+4{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{e}^{4\\theta }}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{e}^{2\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}{e}^{2\\theta }{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}({e}^{\\pi }-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172288 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06dd37ec-93a9-5b21-89e4-561d7cf50bec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{4-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256841} {:answer/id #uuid "0a8e4a81-ce37-5a0a-bf71-1d9989cbe036" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256881} {:answer/id #uuid "297e7d94-4a80-565f-88bd-e5215df2ba8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5+4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257151} {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} {:answer/id #uuid "da8ca18d-520e-5091-a63b-d25c9509aa0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e711455-77c9-5616-92bb-d797cf1c8968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257454} :answer-field/id #uuid "96b3878a-36da-5c94-8d4e-f1633afbca83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259297}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c8bb8d5-c684-5d53-8ebb-fc2e85b6f1e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-123355" :question/problem "![](images/8f/8fbf4c3074641b18d12dc7372378550f4c37e0b9b81843c2e57629c891a59263.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi ,$ shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{1-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{5-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172289 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa492a8-e07a-522a-bf45-34af18c7a267" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{2} θ\\tan^{2} θdθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168598} {:answer/id #uuid "1813a2d0-d5bd-52b4-99b5-efcb1d2a7640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168599} {:answer/id #uuid "2aba48aa-c769-5c6a-9e07-f0c67be25a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + 9\\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168600} {:answer/id #uuid "55f60188-6938-5f92-aae8-dedef3328cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{9 + \\tan^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a3ab0-69fc-59cc-bc73-08e310482454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\sec^{3} θ\\sqrt{1 + \\sec^{2} θ}dθ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168602}] :answer-field/id #uuid "d4363fdf-f405-59b8-b8f3-f8bb7ba8806f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186168597} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} {:answer/id #uuid "94ab8694-5f19-5ea3-8d98-092aec26cb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405025} {:answer/id #uuid "a57cacb1-b76d-579c-8c67-b4527f5232ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{9+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405026} {:answer/id #uuid "1eabb9a5-ecc0-5827-9b8a-fa684cf58678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405027} {:answer/id #uuid "9f675dbc-4798-5a7f-b170-06e1c7c60f24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d61aec87-e699-59eb-980b-a6a6cf0bb0f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9492d74f-ea7c-56c6-a9a7-679ef961d276" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186405023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f58568c3-ce47-5162-ad81-da8c754e43d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-123439" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions is obtained by using the pointwise polar arc-length integrand for ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ for $-\\pi /2<\\theta<\\pi /2$? Interpret the endpoint integrals as improper integrals." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r={sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression for our line integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{({sec}^{3}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(3{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta +9{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{sec}^{6}⁡\\theta (1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta })\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{sec}^{3}⁡\\theta \\sqrt{1+9{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese endpoint integrals are improper and diverge to $+\\infty.$ The requested expression is determined by the pointwise arc-length integrand on $-\\pi/2<\\theta<\\pi/2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172290 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477217b2-25da-5eb5-bf11-b8dba3a034f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257407} {:answer/id #uuid "47c32564-aebd-58a5-91d4-f60e41bdce79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257410} {:answer/id #uuid "8b2954a9-a57d-5b93-b489-ad2d5cb06b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} {:answer/id #uuid "d940cf3f-dda0-5356-bac1-c5452d47f35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258684} {:answer/id #uuid "ed844e7d-d658-557e-a456-73ff91011d22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258836}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b2954a9-a57d-5b93-b489-ad2d5cb06b81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258004} :answer-field/id #uuid "343e4e11-bb27-5f39-9989-a4e9e01a1124" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "474b9c27-587d-5510-bc2c-fbc91f7d6903" :question/math-academy-id "q-123623" :question/problem "Calculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path along the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta $ for $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates, the arc length element $\\text{d}s$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSo, for a polar curve defined as $r=r(\\theta )$ for ${\\theta }_{1}\\le \\theta \\le {\\theta }_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, we evaluate our line integral as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta (1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{4}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3}{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4c9cc4f-a3ca-5b22-a9a9-481f2fe6587a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170354} {:answer/id #uuid "19e0b67a-24a4-5072-bc52-060f2a5fd744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170355} {:answer/id #uuid "440685a2-ae29-51f9-8634-84a784c93861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Exp(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170356} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc6ca66-e120-5fa2-af91-108a0e973a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Γ(3, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170357} {:answer/id #uuid "527d25dd-2812-5217-95d6-3f3360984385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼Γ(5, 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170358}] :answer-field/id #uuid "780b21cd-97a0-52a4-bf45-d7da397d0e62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170353}] :question/id #uuid "c303e8fd-873c-5da3-9aad-43cbd74c4762" :question/math-academy-id "q-240417" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_{1}∼Γ(2, 3)$ and $Y_{2}∼Exp(3)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $Exp(λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $(\\frac{λ}{λ - t})^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172478 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8dc57029-131f-588c-9f91-58ab234e3f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170360} {:answer/id #uuid "d0542e21-35b1-53e0-a6ed-da09799995be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170361} {:answer/id #uuid "2807f281-5fb0-5273-899b-5261be151ce3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170362} {:answer/id #uuid "45bcc294-2db6-5894-9d63-4f31f990f756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170363} {:answer/id #uuid "61f362e8-9d29-5528-b84b-46263a51f05c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170364}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3a7c631e-0cc5-5c10-900d-bf6fa7291a87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170359}] :question/id #uuid "495b3383-98ab-5ca8-bd90-0819e8534e8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-24909" :question/problem "What is $\\sin (\\arccos (\\frac{1}{2}))$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87cb0385-7440-5272-98a7-928a22fd8042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.670" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485112} :answer-field/id #uuid "95f02d2d-c10d-5ca4-b6d9-0139b28173a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13a56b51-6b01-5125-b67e-75c6aab58d86" :question/math-academy-id "q-253892" :question/problem "At a particular helpdesk, customer waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $6$ customers per hour. If a customer has already waited for $6$ minutes, what is the probability that they will need to wait at least $4$ more minutes for their call to be answered? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Since waiting times occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next call is answered using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the customer's waiting time, in minutes. Since the help desk serves $6$ customers per hour on average (or $1$ customer every $10$ minutes), the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ customers per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ $s=6,$ and $t=4.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>6+4\\,|\\,X>6) & =P(X>4) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/10)\\cdot (4)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2/5} \\\\  & ≈0.670.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172543 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d052de8a-4f04-5e04-b975-532c813f9c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170884} {:answer/id #uuid "10ff75c3-daa4-53f3-8882-8cf83b4ba9be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(1, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170885} {:answer/id #uuid "4b146c59-eb19-5c1a-9ad6-dee14307372d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170886} {:answer/id #uuid "77248f77-5b52-5bbb-9490-c54f729b58b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170887} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb24657-c3dd-53ab-a504-11156db31320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y∼N(0, \\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170888}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a0caaede-533a-56f9-b7b6-2d7ce4c9d280" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186170883}] :question/id #uuid "2c68225f-88f7-5039-acc8-1a1fd40fb051" :question/math-academy-id "q-256397" :question/problem "Consider the independent random variables $Y_{1}∼N(0, 1)$ and $Y_{2}∼N(0, 1)$. What is the distribution of $Y = Y_{1} + Y_{2}$?\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results.*\n\n| Distribution | $M(t)$ | $t$-domain |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $U[a, b]$ | $\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{ {e}^{t b} - {e}^{t a}}{t ( b - a )}} , \\hspace{1em} & t \\ne  0 \\\\ 1 , \\hspace{2em} & t = 0\\end{array} \\right.$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $N(μ, σ^{2})$ | $\\exp (μt + \\frac{1}{2}σ^{2}t^{2})$ | $t ∈ R$ |\n| $χ^{2}(k)$ | $(1 - 2t)^{-k/2}$ | $t < \\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $Exp(λ)$ | $\\frac{λ}{λ - t}$ | $t < λ$ |\n| $Γ(α, λ)$ | $(\\frac{λ}{λ - t})^{α}$ | $t < λ$ |"} {:db/id